   "id": "concept2",
   "type": "concept",
   "title": {
    "en": "Encoder and priority encoder"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "An encoder does the reverse: $2^n$ input lines, only one active at a time, give an n-bit code. In an 8:3 (octal to binary) encoder, $A_2 = D_4 + D_5 + D_6 + D_7$, $A_1 = D_2 + D_3 + D_6 + D_7$, $A_0 = D_1 + D_3 + D_5 + D_7$: just three OR gates. A decimal to BCD encoder has 10 inputs and 4 outputs. If two inputs are active together a simple encoder gives a wrong code; a priority encoder (e.g. 74148, 8:3; 74147, decimal to BCD) outputs the code of the highest-priority input."
   },
   "points": [
    {
     "en": "2ⁿ inputs → n outputs"
    },
    {
     "en": "Octal to binary: three OR gates"
    },
    {
     "en": "Priority encoder: highest-numbered active input wins"
    }
   ],
